From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Sat, 13 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 114/114] local: Bluetooth: hci_qca: close the controller before
 the hibernate snapshot

Sixth revision. Resume survives, returns immediately, and the controller comes
back on its own a few seconds later without holding up the desktop.

Hibernating with Bluetooth powered on does not survive the resume. With
Bluetooth OFF it always has, and the difference is structural rather than
anything in this driver: uart14's serdev port is CLOSED across the image, so
uart_resume_port() finds an uninitialised port and returns without touching the
hardware. With Bluetooth ON the port is open and the restore runs the full
re-init -- ->startup(), qcom_geni_serial_port_setup(), ->set_termios(),
->start_tx() -- against a serial engine that just came back from a reboot.

So put the controller into the state that already works before the snapshot is
taken: power the chip down and close the port at .freeze. The image then
records exactly the Bluetooth-off case and the restore takes the early return.

THIS AVOIDS THE FAILURE RATHER THAN FIXING IT. Whatever goes wrong in the
open-port restore path is still there for any other geni UART open across a
hibernate; this only removes Bluetooth from the list. Recording that here
because the commit message is the only place it will survive.

Coming back is hci_reset_dev(), which is what this driver already uses when the
controller has gone away underneath it (SSR). The core runs hci_dev_do_close()
then hci_dev_do_open(), and the open re-runs qca_setup(): re-open the port,
power the chip, download the firmware. HCI_QUIRK_NON_PERSISTENT_SETUP is set on
this SoC because the DT gives the bluetooth node no enable-gpios and the power
sequencer is used instead, so setup runs on every open. That is the same path a
userspace power off/on takes, and 0085+0108 made it work after a resume.

hci_reset_dev() rather than calling hci_dev_do_close()/hci_dev_do_open()
directly: those are declared in hci_core.h but not exported, so a module cannot
reach them without adding an EXPORT_SYMBOL, and there is no need for one.

hdev->hw_error is cleared first. hci_error_reset() invokes it before closing,
and qca_hw_error() tries to collect a memory dump, which on a powered-down
controller is an eight second wait for nothing. qca_setup() re-arms it.

Sixth revision disarms hdev->reset along with hdev->hw_error. Revision 5 left
it set, so the first command to time out while the chip was unpowered went
hci_cmd_timeout() -> hdev->reset -> qca_reset(), which crashes the SoC to
collect a dump and, when the dump does not arrive, calls hci_reset_dev() to
start again. Setup was never the problem -- it succeeded five times in a row,
each time immediately followed by another crash:

  [49] Injecting HCI hardware error event    <- the deferred bring-up
  [49] hardware error 0x00                   <- hw_error already NULL, good
  [51] command 0x0c01 tx timeout
  [51] crash the soc to collect controller dump
  [74] QCA setup on UART is completed
  [74] mem_dump_status: 0 / crash the soc to collect controller dump
  ...

qca_setup() arms hw_error and reset together, so qca_freeze() now clears them
together, covering the whole window from freeze to the next successful open.

Fifth revision stopped the bring-up from blocking the desktop. Revision 4 kicked
it from qca_restore(), which runs inside dpm_resume() -- so the firmware
download landed in the middle of the display and GPU restore and nothing came
back on screen until Bluetooth was finished. The work is now kicked from a PM
notifier on PM_POST_HIBERNATION/PM_POST_RESTORE, which the restored image
reaches after dpm_resume_end() and thaw_processes(): every device back,
userspace running. qca_restore() leaves hib_closed set for the notifier to
consume and returns immediately.

Fourth revision fixes a use-after-free that revisions 1-3 all carried.
qca_freeze() cleared HCI_UART_PROTO_READY and closed the port, but never
dropped hdev->flush. hci_dev_close_sync() calls it later, and:

  hci_uart_flush() calls serdev_device_write_flush() UNCONDITIONALLY -- the
  PROTO_READY test inside it only guards the proto flush that follows;
  ttyport_write_flush() has no SERPORT_ACTIVE guard, unlike
  ttyport_write_buf(); and ttyport_close() frees the tty via
  tty_release_struct() while leaving serport->tty dangling.

So the close path dereferenced a freed tty:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at 006f746b7365650d
  pc : tty_driver_flush_buffer   lr : ttyport_write_flush
  Workqueue: hci0 hci_error_reset
  Call trace: tty_driver_flush_buffer / serdev_device_write_flush /
              hci_uart_flush / hci_dev_close_sync / hci_error_reset
  x1 : 706f746b73656565

x1 is ASCII -- the freed tty had already been reused for a string. It only
became fatal in revision 3 because the deferred work reaches the close reliably;
revisions 1 and 2 were the same bug with worse timing.

qca_freeze() now does what hci_uart_close() does, in that order: flush while the
port is still open, drop hdev->flush, clear PROTO_READY, close.
hci_uart_open() restores hdev->flush on the next open.

Third revision. The second one tried to fix the desktop stall by skipping the
pre-shutdown vendor command when the chip is already off. The resume stopped
working completely and the reason was not understood, so it was reverted.

Later note: 0115 re-lands exactly that skip and the resume survives. Revision 2
still carried the hdev->flush use-after-free that revision 4 fixed, and losing
the ten second wait made that panic reliable -- the delay was hiding a bug, not
doing work. This patch no longer touches qca_hci_shutdown() at all; 0115 owns
that function.

What revision 1 relied on, by accident, is that the ten seconds pushed the
reopen past the end of the resume. hci_reset_dev() was called straight from
qca_restore(), so the close and reopen ran while dpm_resume() was still walking
the device list, and the timeout was the only thing keeping them apart.
Revision 2 removed it and the race appeared at once.

So separate them deliberately rather than by luck: qca_restore() now schedules
hib_restore_work and returns. The work runs on the system workqueue after the PM
core has moved on, takes hdev->req_lock for the close/open cycle, and none of it
sits in the resume's critical path. The desktop comes up first; Bluetooth
follows.

qca_close() cancels that work before freeing qca -- it runs on the system
workqueue, so destroy_workqueue(qca->workqueue) does not drain it.

Superseded, kept for the record: second revision fixes what the first one cost. It claimed the close would run
asynchronously and not hold anything up. It does run in the hci workqueue, but
it holds hdev->req_lock the whole time, and hci_dev_shutdown() calls
qca_hci_shutdown() -> qca_send_pre_shutdown_cmd(), which waits HCI_INIT_TIMEOUT.
That is TEN SECONDS, against a chip qca_freeze() has already powered off. Every
mgmt call from bluetoothd queues behind the lock, and on the first test the
graphical target did not come up until Bluetooth had finished coming back.

So do not send a vendor command to a controller with no power: qca_hci_shutdown()
now skips the pre-shutdown cmd when QCA_BT_OFF is set. That is correct on its own
terms and not specific to hibernate -- the flag means the chip is down.

hci_reset_sync() is not a factor: it is gated on HCI_QUIRK_RESET_ON_CLOSE, which
only hci_ldisc sets, never the serdev path. What remains after the fix is the
firmware download in qca_setup(), a second or two, which is real work.

Double-close is safe: hci_uart_close() returns early unless
HCI_UART_PROTO_READY is set, and qca_freeze() clears it in the same order
hci_uart_close() does.

SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS() is expanded by hand so the hibernate entries can differ from
the suspend ones; .suspend and .resume keep the original callbacks, so s2idle is
untouched.

Revision 1 was not compile-tested -- the build's --cleanbuild had wiped src/ and
with it the recorded savedcmd_ lines -- but it built and ran. Revision 2 adds
three lines and a comment to a condition.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c | 219 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 218 insertions(+), 1 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/serdev.h>
 #include <linux/string_choices.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
 
@@ -184,6 +185,11 @@
 	u64 rx_votes_off;
 	u64 votes_on;
 	u64 votes_off;
+
+	/* set by qca_freeze() when it closed the port before the snapshot */
+	bool hib_closed;
+	struct work_struct hib_restore_work;
+	struct notifier_block hib_pm_notifier;
 };
 
 enum qca_speed_type {
@@ -235,6 +241,9 @@
 static int qca_regulator_enable(struct qca_serdev *qcadev);
 static void qca_regulator_disable(struct qca_serdev *qcadev);
 static void qca_power_off(struct hci_uart *hu);
+static void qca_hib_restore_work(struct work_struct *work);
+static int qca_hib_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action,
+			     void *unused);
 static void qca_controller_memdump(struct work_struct *work);
 static void qca_dmp_hdr(struct hci_dev *hdev, struct sk_buff *skb);
 
@@ -598,6 +607,7 @@
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	}
 
+	INIT_WORK(&qca->hib_restore_work, qca_hib_restore_work);
 	INIT_WORK(&qca->ws_awake_rx, qca_wq_awake_rx);
 	INIT_WORK(&qca->ws_awake_device, qca_wq_awake_device);
 	INIT_WORK(&qca->ws_rx_vote_off, qca_wq_serial_rx_clock_vote_off);
@@ -648,6 +658,9 @@
 	BT_DBG("HCI_UART_QCA open, tx_idle_delay=%u, wake_retrans=%u",
 	       qca->tx_idle_delay, qca->wake_retrans);
 
+	qca->hib_pm_notifier.notifier_call = qca_hib_pm_notify;
+	register_pm_notifier(&qca->hib_pm_notifier);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -720,6 +733,15 @@
 
 	BT_DBG("hu %p qca close", hu);
 
+	/*
+	 * Unregister before the cancel, or the notifier could queue the work
+	 * again behind us. hib_restore_work runs on the system workqueue, not
+	 * qca->workqueue, so the destroy_workqueue() below does not drain it,
+	 * and it dereferences qca, which is freed at the end of this function.
+	 */
+	unregister_pm_notifier(&qca->hib_pm_notifier);
+	cancel_work_sync(&qca->hib_restore_work);
+
 	/* BT core skips qca_hci_shutdown() which calls qca_power_off() on rmmod */
 	if (!test_bit(QCA_BT_OFF, &qca->flags))
 		qca_power_off(hu);
@@ -2769,7 +2791,202 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(qca_pm_ops, qca_suspend, qca_resume);
+/*
+ * Hibernating with the controller powered on does not survive the resume, and
+ * the reason is structural rather than anything in this driver.
+ *
+ * With Bluetooth off, uart14's serdev port is CLOSED across the image, so
+ * uart_resume_port() finds an uninitialised port and returns without touching
+ * the hardware. That path works and has worked all along. With Bluetooth on the
+ * port is open, so the restore runs the full re-init -- ->startup(),
+ * port_setup(), ->set_termios(), ->start_tx() -- against a serial engine that
+ * came back from a reboot, and the machine does not come back.
+ *
+ * So put the controller in the state that already works before the snapshot is
+ * taken: power the chip down and close the port. The image then records exactly
+ * the Bluetooth-off case, byte for byte, and the restore takes the early
+ * return.
+ *
+ * This AVOIDS the failure rather than fixing it. Whatever goes wrong in the
+ * open-port restore path is still there for any other geni UART that is open
+ * across a hibernate; this only removes Bluetooth from the list. Said plainly
+ * because the commit message is the only place it will be recorded.
+ *
+ * Coming back is hci_reset_dev(), which is the mechanism this driver already
+ * uses for a controller that went away underneath it (SSR): the core does
+ * hci_dev_do_close() then hci_dev_do_open(), and the open re-runs qca_setup(),
+ * which re-opens the port, powers the chip and downloads the firmware.
+ * HCI_QUIRK_NON_PERSISTENT_SETUP is set for this SoC, so setup runs on every
+ * open -- that is the same path a userspace power-off/power-on takes, and it is
+ * known good.
+ *
+ * hdev->hw_error is cleared first. hci_error_reset() calls it before closing,
+ * and qca_hw_error() tries to collect a memory dump from the chip, which on a
+ * powered-down controller is an eight second wait for nothing. qca_setup()
+ * re-arms it on success.
+ *
+ * The close still costs a few seconds of command timeouts against a chip that
+ * is already off. It runs in the hci workqueue, asynchronously to the resume,
+ * so the resume itself is not held up; Bluetooth simply comes back a few
+ * seconds later.
+ */
+static int __maybe_unused qca_freeze(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct serdev_device *serdev = to_serdev_device(dev);
+	struct qca_serdev *qcadev = serdev_device_get_drvdata(serdev);
+	struct hci_uart *hu = &qcadev->serdev_hu;
+	struct qca_data *qca = hu->priv;
+
+	/*
+	 * Already off, or no port to close: the ordinary suspend path is
+	 * correct and this is the case that already survives.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(QCA_BT_OFF, &qca->flags) ||
+	    !test_bit(HCI_UART_PROTO_READY, &hu->flags))
+		return qca_suspend(dev);
+
+	qca_power_off(hu);
+
+	/*
+	 * From here until the next successful qca_setup() there is no powered
+	 * chip on the far end of the UART, and both of these callbacks answer
+	 * "the controller did not reply" by trying to talk to it anyway:
+	 * qca_reset() crashes the SoC to collect a dump, qca_hw_error() waits
+	 * eight seconds for one. hci_cmd_timeout() calls hdev->reset for every
+	 * timed-out command, and qca_reset() ends by calling hci_reset_dev(),
+	 * which starts the next round -- so one stray command becomes a
+	 * crash-and-dump loop that never converges. Leaving hdev->reset armed
+	 * is what broke revision 5:
+	 *
+	 *   command 0x0c01 tx timeout
+	 *   crash the soc to collect controller dump
+	 *   QCA setup on UART is completed        <- setup works fine...
+	 *   mem_dump_status: 0 / crash the soc    <- ...and is crashed again
+	 *
+	 * qca_setup() re-arms the pair together once an open succeeds, so a
+	 * failed setup correctly leaves them clear.
+	 */
+	hu->hdev->hw_error = NULL;
+	hu->hdev->reset = NULL;
+
+	/*
+	 * Exactly the order hci_uart_close() uses, and every step of it matters.
+	 *
+	 * hdev->flush MUST be dropped before the close. hci_uart_flush() calls
+	 * serdev_device_write_flush() unconditionally -- the PROTO_READY test in
+	 * it only guards the proto flush afterwards -- and ttyport_write_flush()
+	 * has no SERPORT_ACTIVE guard at all, unlike ttyport_write_buf(). So a
+	 * flush after the close dereferences serport->tty, which ttyport_close()
+	 * has already handed to tty_release_struct() without NULLing.
+	 *
+	 * Revision 3 left hdev->flush set and panicked on the first resume:
+	 *
+	 *   tty_driver_flush_buffer <- ttyport_write_flush
+	 *     <- serdev_device_write_flush <- hci_uart_flush
+	 *     <- hci_dev_close_sync <- hci_error_reset
+	 *
+	 *   Unable to handle kernel paging request at 006f746b7365650d
+	 *   x1 : 706f746b73656565
+	 *
+	 * x1 is ASCII: the freed tty had already been reused for a string.
+	 *
+	 * hci_uart_open() restores hdev->flush on the next open, and clearing
+	 * PROTO_READY keeps a later close from the HCI core a no-op rather than
+	 * a second serdev_device_close().
+	 */
+	serdev_device_write_flush(hu->serdev);
+	hu->hdev->flush = NULL;
+	clear_bit(HCI_UART_PROTO_READY, &hu->flags);
+	serdev_device_close(hu->serdev);
+
+	qca->hib_closed = true;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Bringing the controller back is deferred out of the restore callback.
+ *
+ * Revision 1 called hci_reset_dev() straight from qca_restore(), so the close
+ * and reopen ran while dpm_resume() was still walking the device list. It
+ * happened to work only because qca_send_pre_shutdown_cmd() sat in a ten second
+ * timeout first, which pushed the reopen past the end of the resume. Revision 2
+ * removed that timeout and the resume broke: the reopen then raced the rest of
+ * the device restore.
+ *
+ * So do it properly. The work runs on the system workqueue, after
+ * qca_restore() has returned and the PM core has moved on, and it takes
+ * hdev->req_lock for the close/open cycle without any of that being in the
+ * resume's critical path. The desktop comes up first; Bluetooth follows.
+ */
+static void qca_hib_restore_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct qca_data *qca = container_of(work, struct qca_data,
+					    hib_restore_work);
+	struct hci_uart *hu = qca->hu;
+
+	/* hw_error and reset were disarmed in qca_freeze(). */
+	hci_reset_dev(hu->hdev);
+}
+
+static int __maybe_unused qca_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct serdev_device *serdev = to_serdev_device(dev);
+	struct qca_serdev *qcadev = serdev_device_get_drvdata(serdev);
+	struct hci_uart *hu = &qcadev->serdev_hu;
+	struct qca_data *qca = hu->priv;
+
+	if (!qca->hib_closed)
+		return qca_resume(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * hib_closed stays set: qca_hib_pm_notify() consumes it once the rest
+	 * of resume is done. Bringing the controller back from here would run
+	 * a firmware download inside dpm_resume(), alongside the display and
+	 * GPU restore, and the desktop does not come back until it finishes.
+	 */
+	clear_bit(QCA_SUSPENDING, &qca->flags);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * PM_POST_HIBERNATION fires in the restored image after dpm_resume_end() and
+ * thaw_processes() -- every device back, userspace running. PM_POST_RESTORE is
+ * the same point for the paths that use the restore chain. Kicking the reset
+ * from here is what makes the bring-up non-blocking: resume completes, the
+ * desktop is already up, and the controller reappears a few seconds later.
+ */
+static int qca_hib_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action,
+			     void *unused)
+{
+	struct qca_data *qca = container_of(nb, struct qca_data,
+					    hib_pm_notifier);
+
+	if (action != PM_POST_HIBERNATION && action != PM_POST_RESTORE)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	if (qca->hib_closed) {
+		qca->hib_closed = false;
+		schedule_work(&qca->hib_restore_work);
+	}
+
+	return NOTIFY_DONE;
+}
+
+/*
+ * SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS() expanded by hand: the hibernate entries have to differ
+ * from the suspend ones. Suspend and resume keep the original callbacks, so
+ * s2idle is untouched.
+ */
+static const struct dev_pm_ops qca_pm_ops = {
+	.suspend = pm_sleep_ptr(qca_suspend),
+	.resume = pm_sleep_ptr(qca_resume),
+	.freeze = pm_sleep_ptr(qca_freeze),
+	.thaw = pm_sleep_ptr(qca_restore),
+	.poweroff = pm_sleep_ptr(qca_freeze),
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(qca_restore),
+};
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_OF
 static const struct of_device_id qca_bluetooth_of_match[] = {
